-- brunerie-comparison (2026-11-17): the machine beside Cubical Agda on the same definition, on one
-- machine, every number from repeated interleaved runs with its distribution.
-- What is compared. The typed port (experiments/brunerie/typed.cub with run-typed.cub, 260 checked
-- declarations) is Ljungström's brunerie' of Cubical/Homotopy/Group/Pi4S3/BrunerieExperiments.agda
-- (agda/cubical at cd8c842d, 2023-02-01 "Prepare for Agda 2.6.3", the library of the paper's date;
-- the file at master differs only by a --safe option and a hiding clause): there brunerie' = g10 (g9
-- (g8 λ i j → f7' λ k → η₃ .fst (push (loop i) (loop j) k))) with g8 = mapΩrefl encodeTruncS², g9 =
-- encodeTruncS¹, g10 = SetTrunc.rec isSetℤ (idfun ℤ), f7' = f7stuff.encode north; ours is brunerie =
-- g10 (encodeTruncS1 g8) with g8 = λ i → encodeTruncS2 (λ j → F i j), the same composition with g9
-- and the λs written in (its own K2 = the 4-truncation of S2 as a higher inductive type, CodeK over
-- the suspension of S2 by ua (K2eq a), the Hopf fibration HopfS2). It is not the figure the paper
-- times. Ljungström and Mörtberg, "Formalizing π₄(S³) ≅ ℤ/2ℤ and Computing a Brunerie Number in
-- Cubical Agda" (LICS 2023, arXiv 2302.00151) time "optimised β₃", computerIsoη₃ : fst computer η₃'
-- ≡ -2 of Cubical/Homotopy/Group/Pi4S3/DirectProof.agda, Part 2 ("one of these normalises, in just
-- under 4 seconds on a regular laptop, to −2"), and say β₂ "fails to normalise in reasonable time";
-- brunerie' is the experiments file's variant (its comment: "Computing it takes ~1s"). Both were run.
-- How it was measured. One machine, this container: 4 virtual x86-64 cores (AVX-512), 16 GB, Linux
-- 6.18, nothing else running, the two systems' runs interleaved in rounds. Agda 2.6.3 (Ubuntu 24.04's
-- package): the library's interfaces built once (BrunerieExperiments and its 138 modules, 20,780
-- lines, 2 min 11 s; DirectProof and 90 more, 22,018 lines, 4 min 56 s), then a one-line test module
-- re-checked each run with its own interface removed: `test : ℤ; test = brunerie'` loads without
-- normalising; `test : brunerie' ≡ -2; test = refl` loads and normalises (the proof is by computation);
-- the same two for fst computer η₃'. The machine: build/cubical at e14aaba1, CUBICAL_NOCACHE=1
-- CUBICAL_WORKERS=0, lib/core.cub alone (load and check the core), with typed.cub (load and check the
-- port), with run-typed.cub (and compute the number); the scripts are a loop of `time` around each.
-- Results, 12 rounds each, wall seconds (minimum, lower quartile, median, upper quartile, maximum):
-- Agda brunerie' load 3.59, 3.67, 3.93, 4.00, 4.39; load and normalise 4.15, 4.37, 4.68, 4.79, 4.93:
-- normalising brunerie' is about 0.75 s (difference of medians; the quartile ranges do not overlap).
-- Agda fst computer η₃' load 7.76, 7.80, 8.25, 8.32, 9.24; load and normalise 9.38, 9.70, 9.98, 10.25,
-- 10.79: normalising β₃ is about 1.7 s here (the paper's 4 s was Agda of February 2023 on a laptop).
-- The machine: core alone 1.33, 1.42, 1.48, 1.61, 1.73; core and the port 3.14, 3.23, 3.45, 3.59,
-- 4.20; core, the port and the computation 3.31, 3.36, 3.51, 3.65, 3.75. So loading and checking the
-- port's 260 declarations is about 2.0 s. The computation's own time cannot be read from these: the
-- run with it and the run without it differ by 0.06 s at the median while each varies by about
-- 0.3 s between repetitions, so twelve repetitions cannot tell 0.06 s from 0 s. It was measured
-- directly instead (2026-11-18, at ab3ac3301, where the core of that day makes the whole run about
-- 2.4 s and the reading 358,691 steps): CUBICAL_PROGRESS=20000 makes the machine print its step
-- count every 20,000 steps, strace timestamps every such line and the result's line, and the
-- reading is the last K steps of the run's N (K from the result line, N from the summary), so it
-- begins where the counter crosses N−K (interpolated between the two lines around it) and ends at
-- the write of its result, printed after it. Twelve runs: 0.145, 0.145, 0.149, 0.160, 0.186 s
-- (minimum, lower quartile, median, upper quartile, maximum), mean 0.155 s. The step-share estimate
-- that stood here before (4.1% of the run's user time, 0.14 s, assuming every step costs alike) was
-- an inference, not a measurement; it happens to agree.
-- Agda's normalisation measured directly (2026-11-20, Avik: "what's the standard for statistical
-- significance?"): agda --profile=all accounts the check of `refl : brunerie' ≡ -2` under
-- Typing.CheckRHS, twelve runs interleaved with the loading module: 634, 690 (median), 787 ms,
-- mean 691 ms, standard deviation 38 ms, standard error 11 ms. The difference of the two whole-run
-- means (4.594 − 3.927 = 0.667 s) has a standard error of 0.103 s (the two standard errors, 0.070
-- and 0.076, in quadrature), so by difference the ratio to ours was only known to lie between about
-- 3 and 6; by the direct number it is 691 ms against 155 ms (ours: mean 0.155 s, sd 0.013, se
-- 0.004), 4.5 with a 95% interval of about 4.0 to 5.0. The standard: a quantity is reported with
-- its standard error and a difference is significant when it exceeds about twice the standard error
-- of the difference (the 95% convention); the number of runs needed is set by the spread against
-- the effect, not by a fixed count, and the right instrument (Agda's own phase accounting, the step
-- counter under strace) did more than a hundredfold more runs would have: the error of a mean falls
-- only as the square root of the count. What is not significant in this node's data, and is said
-- so: the whole-run difference with and without the computation (0.044 s against a standard error
-- of 0.097 s) and the memo on against off (evaluator-speed); to see a 1% effect on a 3.3 s run with
-- its 0.2 s spread would take about 150 runs an arm.
-- A hundred runs of each, interleaved (2026-11-20, Avik: "more runs wouldn't give more confidence in
-- anything?"): ours 0.141, 0.152, 0.159, 0.170, 0.221 s (minimum, quartiles, median, maximum), mean
-- 0.1625, sd 0.015, se 0.0015; Agda's Typing.CheckRHS 0.656, 0.695, 0.720, 0.746, 0.906 s, mean
-- 0.7275, sd 0.046, se 0.0046. Ratio of means 4.48, 95% interval 4.38 to 4.58: the same conclusion
-- as twelve runs gave, the interval five times tighter. The memo at the same count (evaluator-speed):
-- 100 pairs on Brunerie 2.689 against 2.693 s (se 0.017 and 0.019), 30 pairs on the library 44.20
-- against 44.18 s (se 0.27 and 0.19): no effect above about 2% and 1.5%.
-- Against which Agda (2026-11-20, Avik: "and you used a 3 year old version because you're a fucking
-- geniusS?"). Everything above is Agda 2.6.3, the version apt installs, with the library at the
-- paper's date. That is the wrong comparison for a claim about the present: the claim must be
-- against the current release. The current cubical library (master, 2026-11-19) states Agda v2.8.0;
-- the latest Agda release is 2.9.0. Both were built from Hackage here (GHC 9.4.7, cabal 3.8.1.0,
-- Hackage's default flags, which include optimise-heavily), and the current library checks under
-- both (its two modules' interfaces: under 2.9.0 in 63 s and 77 s, under 2.8.0.2 in 123 s and 179 s;
-- under 2.6.3 with the 2023 library they took 131 s and 296 s). Results, 2026-11-21, same method
-- as above, machine otherwise idle, 100 rounds with the three measurements interleaved in each:
-- our computation 0.136, 0.146, 0.151, 0.159, 0.214 s (minimum, quartiles, median, maximum), mean
-- 0.1542, sd 0.012, se 0.0012; Agda 2.9.0's Typing.CheckRHS 0.217, 0.225, 0.231, 0.238, 0.311 s,
-- mean 0.2363, sd 0.018, se 0.0018; Agda 2.8.0.2's 0.309, 0.324, 0.334, 0.341, 0.467 s, mean
-- 0.3361, sd 0.021, se 0.0021. Ratios of means: against 2.9.0, 1.53 (95% interval 1.50 to 1.57);
-- against 2.8.0.2, 2.18 (2.13 to 2.23); against 2.6.3 (above), 4.48. Whole process under the
-- current versions, 12 rounds, medians: 2.9.0 loads brunerie' in 2.13 s and computes it in 2.38 s
-- (its CheckRHS 241 ms there), loads β₃ in 3.47 s and computes it in 4.07 s (CheckRHS 547 ms);
-- 2.8.0.2: 4.23, 4.58 (332 ms), 7.50, 8.13 s (738 ms). Agda got faster by 3.1 times between 2.6.3
-- and 2.9.0 on this normalisation; the machine's lead over the system that has done this
-- computation is 1.5 times at the current release, not 4.5 and not 25.
-- Why (2026-11-21, Avik: "and do you understand why we're faster?"): not understood; one cause
-- measured, and it runs the other way. The reading's own time under each switch the machine has,
-- thirty interleaved rounds by the step-counter clock: as it is 0.160 s median (mean 0.164, se
-- 0.003, 358,691 steps); the memo table off 0.160 s (on this core the switch no longer gates the
-- cut, so nothing changes); the free-dimension cut off (CUBICAL_NOA8=1) 0.089 s (mean 0.091, se
-- 0.002, 405,568 steps). The cut (net.c, A8: a value's free dimensions walked once it is built and
-- kept on its box, so a substitution of other dimensions is dropped) saves 13% of the steps and
-- costs 45% of the time: its walks (box_fd, fd_cell, fd_port) are 6–8% of the reading's samples and
-- what they feed the rest. Over whole runs, twelve pairs, it changes nothing: Brunerie 2.65 s with
-- against 2.67 s without (se 0.03 and 0.04), the library 45.3 against 45.0 s (se 0.6 and 0.5);
-- every value and verdict identical (262 and 2810 passed, 0 failed, either way), the step and cell
-- counts not. Without the cut the machine computes brunerie' in 0.089 s against Agda 2.9.0's
-- 0.236 s: 2.6 times. Where the reading's time goes, perf samples inside its window (three runs,
-- 519 to 590 samples of the machine each; a third of the window's samples are the kernel's, the
-- cost of strace's timestamps, not the machine's): the interactions (interact, cell, link, link_now,
-- cell_free, flush_frees, pop, lazy_copy) about 20%; the allocator (malloc, free, consolidate,
-- memset) about 15%; the cut's walks 6–8%; building bodies (build_m, run_frame) 3–4%; nothing else
-- above 1%. What would answer the question: each remaining mechanism (operands unrun until read,
-- substitutions held and applied one level at a time, faces as canonical normal forms, the shared
-- graph itself) switched off in turn and the reading timed, as the cut was; and on Agda's side a
-- measure of work common to both, which neither prints. Until then the design facts are
-- candidates, not causes.
-- The cut's two parts apart (2026-11-21, thirty interleaved rounds each): as it was 0.167 s median
-- (mean 0.174, se 0.004, 358,691 steps); the walk of built values off (CUBICAL_NOA8=1) 0.088 s
-- (0.095, 0.003, 405,568 steps); the bound on pending operations off (CUBICAL_NOA8B=1) 0.070 s
-- (0.077, 0.002, 395,608 steps); both off 0.088 s (0.092, 0.002). The bound on pending operations is
-- what costs; with it gone the walk of built values helps. Values and verdicts identical with the
-- bound off (Brunerie 262 passed, the library 2810 passed, 0 failed, either way). On Avik's word
-- ("Sure we can drop it or disable it by default") it is off by default from 2026-11-21 (src/net.c,
-- a8b_on; CUBICAL_A8B=1 turns it on), so the machine's reading as it now stands is the 0.070 s row.
-- What the comparison says, against current Agda. Normalising brunerie': Agda 2.9.0 0.236 s, the
-- machine 0.070 s as it now stands (0.154 s before the bound was turned off), three and a third
-- times; the 1.5 and 2.6 above were the machine as it was and with the whole cut off. Against Agda 2.6.3, which no longer bears on
-- the present: 0.73 s against 0.16 s. The
-- whole process: Agda 3.9 s to load the experiments module from compiled interfaces against the
-- machine's 3.45 s to read and check the core and the port from source with no cache; these are not
-- the same work (Agda's checking of the 138 modules is paid once, outside the timed run).
-- What this changes (Avik, 2026-11-18: "So your work amounts to a bunch of numbers with no
-- significance?"). The handoff of 2026-11-16 set our 0.15 s beside the paper's 4 s, a factor of
-- about 25: that comparison does not hold, and nothing of it is publishable. The 4 s is a different
-- definition (β₃, not brunerie'), a different machine and an Agda three years older; on one machine
-- with the same definition and the same three-year-old Agda the factor was four and a half, and
-- against the current Agda it is one and a half (0.236 s against 0.154 s, both measured directly,
-- a hundred rounds, 95% interval 1.50 to 1.57). What is publishable is exactly that: the same
-- definition, one machine, the current release of the one system that has done the computation,
-- both repeated and interleaved, with the method and the error of each number stated: the machine
-- is ahead of the state of the art on this computation by one and a half times, and the paper's own
-- β₃ (0.55 s in Agda 2.9.0 here) has no number on our side until it is ported;
-- and that the whole-run comparison (3.9 s against 3.45 s) compares loading compiled interfaces
-- with checking from text and says nothing about either evaluator. The claim the project makes
-- in public rests on this node, not on the handoff's figure.
-- Why −2 is trusted. The value is read at the declared type Int of a definition the checker accepted:
-- the checker is lib/check.cub, a hyperactive program the same net runs; the port passes 260 checks
-- with 0 failed, and the machine prints `brunerie = negsuc (suc zero)`. The same value and the same
-- 358,603 steps on four workers (the Makefile's brunerie target runs both); the same value with the
-- memo off (CUBICAL_NOMEMO=1: 405,568 steps, every other line of the output identical). In Agda the
-- same value is brunerie'≡-2 = refl, accepted by its checker.
-- What remains for the paper's own figure. Porting β₃ exactly is porting fst computer η₃': computer is
-- composed of group isomorphisms (π₁S¹→∙ΩS³'≅π₃*S³', the encoding of the 2-truncation, ΩK₂'s base
-- change, π₁S¹→ℤ) whose definitions reach through Cubical.Homotopy.Group.Pi4S3.S3PushoutIso,
-- BlakersMassey, Freudenthal, HopfInvariant, ZCohomology (the ring structure, Gysin) and the algebra
-- modules: the 90 modules and 22,018 lines above the experiments' closure. β₂ (η₂) and the original β
-- (η₁) are in the same file over the same closure. This is the port the handoff asks for, and it is
-- not a session's side job: raised with Avik as the question of the paper itself.
brunerie-comparison : Node
brunerie-comparison = node "The Brunerie number computed by the machine beside current Cubical Agda computing the same definition, on one machine, repeated and interleaved: the measurements with their errors, their method, why −2 is trusted, the machine's lead of one and a half times at Agda 2.9.0, and what porting the paper's own β₃ requires." (cons language-implementation-remainder (cons expose-symbolic-superintelligence nil))
